---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Treadmill Maintenance (2026): The Complete Owner's Schedule";
const description =
  "The jobs that decide whether a treadmill lasts four years or twelve — belt lubrication, tension, tracking and motor care — with a schedule and honest time costs.";

const SCHEDULE = [
  {
    when: "After every session",
    job: "Wipe the console, handrails and deck rails",
    time: "30 seconds",
    why: "Sweat is mildly corrosive and gets into console seams and bolt heads.",
  },
  {
    when: "Weekly",
    job: "Vacuum around and under the machine",
    time: "3 minutes",
    why: "Dust drawn into the motor housing is the single biggest cause of early motor failure.",
  },
  {
    when: "Monthly",
    job: "Check belt tension and tracking",
    time: "5 minutes",
    why: "A drifting belt destroys its own edge against the frame within weeks.",
  },
  {
    when: "Every 3-6 months",
    job: "Lubricate the deck with 100% silicone",
    time: "10 minutes",
    why: "A dry deck multiplies motor load. This is the job that kills treadmills.",
  },
  {
    when: "Every 6 months",
    job: "Check and re-torque frame and upright bolts",
    time: "10 minutes",
    why: "Settled bolts are why a quiet machine develops a hum in year two.",
  },
  {
    when: "Annually",
    job: "Open the motor hood and vacuum the compartment",
    time: "20 minutes",
    why: "Unplug first. Dust on the motor and controller board traps heat.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Guides", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Maintenance", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-maintenance/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How often should you service a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Wipe it down after each session, vacuum around it weekly, check belt tension and tracking monthly, lubricate the deck every three to six months, and vacuum inside the motor hood once a year with the machine unplugged. That is roughly forty minutes of work across a whole year, and it is the difference between a machine that lasts four years and one that lasts twelve.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What happens if you never lubricate a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Friction between the belt and the deck rises steadily, the motor draws more current to hold the same speed, and heat builds in the motor and the speed controller. The usual sequence is a belt that starts to hesitate under foot, then a controller board failure or a burnt-out motor. It is the most common cause of premature treadmill death and the easiest one to avoid.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What lubricant should you use on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "100% silicone, applied under the belt, and nothing else. Never use WD-40, petroleum oils, cooking sprays or general-purpose lubricants — they attack the belt backing and the deck laminate, and some are flammable near a warm motor. Check your manual first, because a few decks ship pre-waxed and must not be lubricated at all.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do you know if a treadmill belt needs tightening?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Walk on it at 3 mph and shift your weight onto one foot. If the belt hesitates or slips momentarily, it is too loose. The other check is to lift the belt at the centre of the deck — you should be able to raise it two to three inches, no more. Over-tightening is a real hazard: it loads the bearings and stretches the belt permanently.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is treadmill maintenance worth doing yourself?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The routine jobs, yes — lubrication, tension, tracking and cleaning need no expertise and no tools beyond the hex key that came in the box. Anything involving the motor, the controller board or mains wiring is a technician's job, and opening the console on a machine still under warranty will usually void it. The line is simple: if it needs a screwdriver on an electrical assembly, stop.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does treadmill maintenance actually extend its life?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Substantially. Owner reports and warranty patterns consistently show maintained machines running eight to twelve years against four to six for neglected ones, and the gap is almost entirely explained by deck lubrication. Given the work amounts to under an hour a year, it is the highest-return thing you can do with a treadmill you already own.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-03",
      dateModified: "2026-09-03",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-03",
    modifiedTime: "2026-09-03",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill maintenance",
      "treadmill lubrication",
      "treadmill belt",
      "treadmill care",
      "home gym",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-maint" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-maint)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Owner's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Treadmill <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Maintenance</span>, Properly
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Under an hour of work a year is the difference between a treadmill
          that lasts four years and one that lasts twelve. Here is the whole
          schedule, what each job actually does, and the three mistakes that
          destroy machines.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#schedule" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Schedule
          </a>
          <a href="#lubrication" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            The Job That Matters Most
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 3, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-red-900">Before anything else:</strong> unplug the
          machine at the wall before you lift a belt, remove a motor hood or put
          a tool anywhere near it. Treadmills store no dangerous charge once
          unplugged, but they do start unexpectedly if a console button is
          knocked while your hands are under the belt. Your manual overrides
          everything on this page — intervals and lubricant type vary by model,
          and a few decks must never be lubricated at all.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Why Maintenance Decides Lifespan</a>
          <a href="#schedule" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The Complete Schedule</a>
          <a href="#lubrication" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Lubrication: The Job That Matters Most</a>
          <a href="#tension" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Belt Tension and Tracking</a>
          <a href="#cleaning" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Cleaning, Dust and Sweat</a>
          <a href="#motor" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Motor, Power and Electronics</a>
          <a href="#moving" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Folding, Moving and Storage</a>
          <a href="#technician" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. What to Leave to a Technician</a>
          <a href="#economics" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. What Neglect Actually Costs</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most treadmills do not wear out. They are killed, slowly, by a dry
          deck — and the owner never knows it is happening, because the failure
          arrives as a dead controller board four years in and looks like bad
          luck. The total work required to prevent that is about forty minutes
          spread across a year.
        </p>

        <div id="why" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why Maintenance Decides Lifespan</h2>
          <p>
            A treadmill is a belt being dragged across a stationary board with a
            person standing on it. Everything else — the motor, the controller,
            the console — exists to keep that happening at a constant speed. So
            the single variable that determines how hard the whole machine works
            is the friction between the belt and the deck, and that friction is
            almost entirely under your control.
          </p>
          <p>
            When a deck is properly lubricated, the motor holds pace with a
            modest current draw and runs cool. As the lubricant depletes,
            friction rises, and the controller compensates by feeding the motor
            more current to maintain the same belt speed. More current means
            more heat, in both the motor windings and the speed controller
            board. Heat is what kills electronics, and it does so cumulatively
            rather than suddenly.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is why the failure mode is so misleading. The owner notices
            nothing for two or three years, then the machine develops a hesitation
            under foot, then one day the controller board fails and the repair
            quote is a third of what the treadmill cost. Nothing dramatic
            happened. The deck simply ran dry eighteen months earlier and the
            electronics have been cooking ever since.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical consequence is that maintenance return is wildly
            asymmetric. Skipping the annual motor-hood vacuum costs you a little.
            Skipping lubrication for two years can cost you the machine. If you
            do exactly one thing from this page, make it the lubrication
            schedule in section three.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="schedule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Complete Schedule</h2>
          <p>
            Intervals below assume normal home use — roughly three to five
            sessions a week. Halve them if the machine runs several hours a day
            or lives somewhere dusty like a garage or a carpeted room.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[640px] w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Interval</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Job</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Time</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Why It Matters</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              SCHEDULE.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? "bg-white" : "bg-gray-50"}>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">{row.when}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">{row.job}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">{row.time}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-500 align-top">{row.why}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Added up, that is well under an hour a year. The reason it does not get
          done is not the time cost — it is that none of these jobs has a
          deadline that announces itself. Nothing goes wrong the week you skip
          them, which is precisely why they get skipped for three years running.
          Put the lubrication date in a calendar and the rest tends to follow.
        </p>

        <div id="lubrication" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Lubrication: The Job That Matters Most</h2>
          <p>
            Deck lubrication sits between the underside of the belt and the top
            of the deck board. It is not for the motor, not for the rollers, and
            not for the hinge. Its entire purpose is to keep the belt sliding
            over the board with as little resistance as possible, which keeps
            motor current — and therefore heat — low.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use 100% silicone and nothing else.</strong> Most
            manufacturers supply a bottle with the machine and sell refills.
            Never use WD-40, motor oil, cooking spray or a general-purpose
            lubricant: petroleum products attack the belt backing and the deck
            laminate, some are flammable near a warm motor, and all of them will
            void a warranty the moment a technician sees the residue.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check whether your deck needs it at all.</strong> A minority
            of machines ship with a pre-waxed or self-lubricating deck that must
            not be lubricated, because adding silicone to a waxed surface makes a
            slurry that increases friction rather than reducing it. Your manual
            will say. Assume nothing on this point.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The method,</strong> in short: unplug the machine, loosen the
            belt slightly if it is very taut, lift one edge at the midpoint of
            the deck, and apply silicone in a line along the underside toward the
            centre — not at the very edges, where it will simply migrate off.
            Repeat on the other side. Then plug in and walk at 2 mph for three to
            five minutes to distribute it evenly. Full step-by-step, including
            how much to use and how to tell when a deck is genuinely dry, is in
            our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not over-apply.</strong> Excess silicone runs off the deck
            and onto the floor, gets flung into the motor compartment, and
            attracts dust that then forms an abrasive paste. More is emphatically
            not better here. If silicone is visibly pooling at the edge of the
            deck or marking the floor, you have used several times too much.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tension" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Belt Tension and Tracking</h2>
          <p>
            These are two different adjustments made with the same two bolts at
            the rear of the machine, and confusing them is how people make things
            worse. <strong>Tension</strong> is how tight the belt is, adjusted by
            turning both rear bolts equally. <strong>Tracking</strong> is whether
            the belt runs centred, adjusted by turning one bolt relative to the
            other.
          </p>
          <p>
            The tension test: walk at around 3 mph and put your weight firmly onto
            one foot. If the belt hesitates or slips for an instant, it is too
            loose — tighten each rear bolt a quarter turn, both sides equally,
            and re-test. The opposite check matters just as much: with the machine
            off, you should be able to lift the belt two to three inches at the
            centre of the deck. If you cannot, it is over-tight, which loads the
            roller bearings, stretches the belt permanently and puts drag on the
            motor.
          </p>
          <p>
            Tracking is simpler than it looks. Run the belt empty at 3 mph and
            watch which way it drifts. If it moves right, turn the right rear
            bolt a quarter turn clockwise; if left, the left bolt. Wait thirty
            seconds between adjustments, because the belt takes time to settle
            and the usual mistake is over-correcting in three quick turns. A belt
            left drifting will grind its own edge against the frame and destroy
            itself in a matter of weeks, so this is worth catching early.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the belt slips even after tensioning, or drifts back off-centre
            within a day of every adjustment, the cause is usually elsewhere — a
            dry deck, a worn belt, a loose drive belt or an uneven floor. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt slipping and tracking guide</a>
            works through those in order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Cleaning, Dust and Sweat</h2>
          <p>
            Dust is the second-biggest killer after friction, and the mechanism
            is straightforward: the motor compartment has a cooling fan, that fan
            pulls room air across the motor and the controller board, and
            whatever is in the room air ends up coating both. A layer of dust on
            a controller board is a thermal blanket on the component you least
            want to insulate.
          </p>
          <p>
            So vacuum around and under the machine weekly. This matters far more
            on carpet than on a hard floor — carpet fibres shed continuously and
            get drawn straight into the housing, which is the main argument for
            putting a treadmill on a mat even when noise is not a concern. Once a
            year, with the machine unplugged, remove the motor hood and vacuum
            the compartment gently. Use a vacuum rather than compressed air,
            which just relocates the dust deeper into the assembly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sweat is the other input. It is mildly corrosive and it collects in
            console seams, around bolt heads and in the gap between the deck and
            the side rails. Wipe the console, handrails and rails after every
            session with a damp cloth — not a soaked one, and never spray cleaner
            directly at a console, where liquid runs straight into the
            electronics. Avoid alcohol-based and ammonia-based cleaners on
            touchscreens, which strip anti-glare coatings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Motor, Power and Electronics</h2>
          <p>
            The motor itself needs nothing from you beyond a clean, cool
            environment. Home treadmill motors are sealed units with no
            user-serviceable parts, and the correct maintenance is to keep dust
            off them and let them run at a sensible current draw — which brings
            you back to lubrication.
          </p>
          <p>
            Power delivery is where owners can do real good or real harm. Plug
            the machine directly into a wall outlet on a circuit it does not share
            with other heavy loads. Avoid extension leads and cheap power strips:
            a treadmill draws a large surge on start-up, and an undersized lead
            produces a voltage sag that the controller reads as a fault, which is
            behind a surprising number of intermittent shutdowns. If you want
            surge protection, use a unit rated for the machine's current draw
            rather than a generic office strip.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where the machine sits matters too. It should be level — a treadmill
            on an uneven floor will fight its own belt tracking indefinitely, and
            no amount of bolt adjustment fixes a twisted frame. Most machines have
            adjustable rear feet for this. Keep it out of unheated garages and
            damp basements where you can: condensation on a cold controller board
            during a temperature swing causes corrosion that never announces
            itself until the board fails.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, use the shutdown the manual specifies. Some machines want
            the power switch off rather than just the console asleep, and leaving
            a controller energised continuously for years is an avoidable stress
            on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moving" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Folding, Moving and Storage</h2>
          <p>
            Folding treadmills add a hinge and a lift assist to the list of things
            that can wear. The assist — hydraulic on most machines — is what
            makes the difference between a fold you do daily and one you do twice
            and abandon. It needs no lubrication, but it does need to be allowed
            to work: forcing a deck down against a hydraulic cylinder rather than
            letting it descend at its own rate is how those cylinders fail. Our
            <a href="/folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding mechanism primer</a>
            covers which designs hold up.
          </p>
          <p>
            Always fold with the machine unplugged and the belt stopped, and check
            the latch has engaged before you walk away. A deck that drops from the
            folded position is the most dangerous failure a home treadmill has,
            and it is nearly always a latch that was not properly seated rather
            than one that broke.
          </p>
          <p>
            Moving a treadmill is a two-person job on anything above entry level,
            and the correct grip is the frame, never the console uprights or the
            handrails. Uprights are bolted, not welded, on most consumer machines,
            and taking the weight of a 200-pound treadmill through them bends the
            mounting plate. Use the transport wheels for distance and lift only to
            clear a threshold.
          </p>
          <p>
            For storage over a season: lubricate before storing rather than after,
            release belt tension slightly so it does not take a set, cover the
            machine to keep dust out, and keep it somewhere dry. Bringing a cold
            machine into a warm room and switching it on immediately is a
            condensation risk — let it reach room temperature first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technician" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What to Leave to a Technician</h2>
          <p>
            The dividing line is simple and worth holding to: mechanical
            adjustments accessible with the supplied hex key are yours;
            anything requiring you to open an electrical assembly is not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do yourself:</strong> lubrication, belt tension and tracking,
            cleaning, re-torquing frame bolts, replacing the safety key, vacuuming
            the motor compartment with the machine unplugged, levelling the feet.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Call someone:</strong> replacing a motor, a controller board
            or a drive belt; diagnosing a burning smell or a tripped breaker;
            anything involving mains wiring; and any repair on a machine still
            under warranty, where opening the console will usually void it
            outright. Manufacturers generally cover parts for one to three years
            and labour for one, so check what you are entitled to before paying
            anybody.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three symptoms mean stop using the machine and get help rather than
            investigating yourself: a burning or hot-plastic smell, a breaker that
            trips when the machine starts, and a belt that continues moving after
            the stop key is pulled. The first two are electrical faults and the
            third is a safety system failure. None of them is a wait-and-see
            situation.
          </p>
          <p>
            For symptoms short of those — a machine that will not start, cuts out
            mid-session, shows an error code or has developed a new noise — work
            through our
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill troubleshooting guide</a>
            first. A useful share of those turn out to be a safety key seated
            badly or a dry deck.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="economics" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What Neglect Actually Costs</h2>
          <p>
            Silicone costs a few pounds a bottle and a bottle lasts a couple of
            years. A replacement controller board is commonly a couple of hundred,
            plus a call-out. A replacement motor is more, and on a mid-range
            machine either repair puts you within sight of the price of a new
            treadmill — which is why so many four-year-old treadmills go to the
            tip rather than to a technician.
          </p>
          <p>
            Set against that, the whole annual maintenance load is under an hour
            and one bottle of silicone. There are very few maintenance decisions
            in any category of household equipment with that return profile, and
            the reason it still gets skipped is purely that nothing bad happens
            immediately.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is a resale argument too. A documented maintenance history and a
            belt in good condition are what separate a second-hand treadmill that
            sells for a reasonable fraction of its price from one nobody wants.
            If you expect to upgrade eventually, the maintenance is partly an
            investment in that.
          </p>
          <p>
            How long a well-maintained machine should actually last, and when
            repair stops making sense against replacement, is the subject of our
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill lifespan guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you service a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wipe it down after each session, vacuum around it weekly, check
                belt tension and tracking monthly, lubricate the deck every three
                to six months, and vacuum inside the motor hood once a year with
                the machine unplugged. That is roughly forty minutes of work
                across a whole year, and it is the difference between a machine
                that lasts four years and one that lasts twelve.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you never lubricate a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Friction between the belt and the deck rises steadily, the motor
                draws more current to hold the same speed, and heat builds in the
                motor and the speed controller. The usual sequence is a belt that
                starts to hesitate under foot, then a controller board failure or
                a burnt-out motor. It is the most common cause of premature
                treadmill death and the easiest one to avoid.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What lubricant should you use on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                100% silicone, applied under the belt, and nothing else. Never use
                WD-40, petroleum oils, cooking sprays or general-purpose
                lubricants — they attack the belt backing and the deck laminate,
                and some are flammable near a warm motor. Check your manual first,
                because a few decks ship pre-waxed and must not be lubricated at
                all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you know if a treadmill belt needs tightening?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Walk on it at 3 mph and shift your weight onto one foot. If the
                belt hesitates or slips momentarily, it is too loose. The other
                check is to lift the belt at the centre of the deck — you should
                be able to raise it two to three inches, no more. Over-tightening
                is a real hazard: it loads the bearings and stretches the belt
                permanently.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is treadmill maintenance worth doing yourself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The routine jobs, yes — lubrication, tension, tracking and
                cleaning need no expertise and no tools beyond the hex key that
                came in the box. Anything involving the motor, the controller
                board or mains wiring is a technician's job, and opening the
                console on a machine still under warranty will usually void it.
                The line is simple: if it needs a screwdriver on an electrical
                assembly, stop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does treadmill maintenance actually extend its life?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Substantially. Owner reports and warranty patterns consistently
                show maintained machines running eight to twelve years against
                four to six for neglected ones, and the gap is almost entirely
                explained by deck lubrication. Given the work amounts to under an
                hour a year, it is the highest-return thing you can do with a
                treadmill you already own.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Treadmill Owners Make</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">These four cause most of the premature failures we see reported, and the first one causes more than the other three combined.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never lubricating the deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A dry deck raises friction, the controller feeds the motor more current to compensate, and both cook slowly for months before anything visible happens. The board or motor then fails and the repair costs a third of a new machine. Lubricate every three to six months with 100% silicone, and put the date in a calendar — nothing about a dry deck announces itself in time.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using WD-40 or any petroleum lubricant</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Petroleum products attack the belt backing and the deck laminate, some are flammable near a warm motor, and the residue voids warranties on sight. Only 100% silicone belongs under a treadmill belt. Check the manual first too: a minority of decks are pre-waxed and adding silicone to them makes a slurry that increases friction instead of reducing it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Over-tightening the belt to stop it slipping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Slipping usually means a dry deck, not a loose belt, and cranking the rear bolts to mask it loads the roller bearings, stretches the belt permanently and adds drag the motor has to overcome. You should still be able to lift the belt two to three inches at the centre of the deck. Lubricate first, then adjust tension a quarter turn at a time.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it on an extension lead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill draws a large surge on start-up, and an undersized lead or cheap power strip produces a voltage sag the controller interprets as a fault. That is behind a great many intermittent shutdowns and mysterious error codes. Plug straight into a wall outlet on a circuit that is not shared with other heavy loads.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

